<th id="S6.T3.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T3.5.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">OpenLVLM-MIA: Instruction Tuning</span></th>
<td id="S6.T3.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T3.5.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.515</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T3.5.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.053</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T3.5.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.0016</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T3.5.7.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.137</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bias in VL-MIA</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large distributional bias was detected in the VL-MIA dataset.
For <span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">img_Flickr</span> (natural images), it achieved an AUROC of 0.949, enabling the identification of member/non-member with an accuracy of 0.813 (at 0.05 FPR) using only image embeddings.
For <span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">img_Dalle</span> (AI-generated images), AUROC=0.729 and TPR@0.05FPR=0.240.
The difference is significant at <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.
MMD and FID are also large; in particular, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">img_Flickr</span> has FID=0.428, indicating distinct distributions.
These results suggest that reported MIA performance on VL-MIA may capture dataset bias rather than true membership.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distributional Alignment in OpenLVLM-MIA</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, OpenLVLM-MIA shows fair distributional alignment.
Across all phases, TPR@0.05FPR stays between 0.053 and 0.095, indicating that the two groups are hard to distinguish even at low FPR. AUROC also remains around 0.5, confirming high distribution consistency.
MMD is at most 0.0022, showing effective bias control.
Differences are significant at <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> for the Vision Encoder and Projector phases, but not for the final Instruction Tuning phase (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>).
This supports fair MIA evaluation enabled by public training data and careful dataset design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Embedding-Space Visualization</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Embedding-Space Visualization ‣ 6.1.3 Results ‣ 6.1 Experiment 1: Distribution Audit ‣ 6 Experimental Evaluation ‣ OpenLVLM-MIA: A Controlled Benchmark Revealing the Limits of Membership Inference Attacks on Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualizes the embedding space in 3D.
Nonlinear dimension reduction methods such as t-SNE and UMAP emphasize local structure, but image embeddings are high-dimensional, making it difficult to visualize separation in 3D space using unsupervised methods.
Therefore, in this study, we quantified separability using supervised dimensionality reduction with membership labels, where the Fisher discriminant axis <math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\propto S_{w}^{-1}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\propto S_{w}^{-1}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> derived from LDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> serves as the first axis.
Furthermore, by applying PCA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> in the residual space orthogonal to this, we constructed the second and third axes, thereby visualizing variations beyond the discriminant axis.
This makes any separation (or its absence) visible.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16295v1/figures/visualize.png" id="S6.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/169;" width="685" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S6.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LDA+PCA 3D visualization of the embedding space. <span id="S6.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dim1</span> is the supervised LDA discriminant axis; <span id="S6.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dim2–3</span> are the top two PCA components in the subspace orthogonal to the LDA axis. VL-MIA shows clear separation, while OpenLVLM-MIA exhibits substantial overlap.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Experiment 2: Benchmark Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Experiment 2, we evaluated the accuracy of existing MIA methods on the proposed benchmark (OpenLVLM-MIA). The experiments revealed that existing methods achieved chance-level AUROC, suggesting they may have detected distribution bias on previous datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.1 </span>Setup</h4>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem Setting</h5>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of MIA is to decide whether a given data point (image or text) was in the model’s training set.
We assume a gray-box setting where the attacker can query the model and access output logits and generated text.
We compute membership scores from logit slices corresponding to the image embedding (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">img</span>), instruction prompt to encourage captioning (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">inst</span>), generated description (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">desp</span>), and their concatenation (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">inst+desp</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Methods</h5>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate ten state-of-the-art MIA methods on LVLMs:
Perplexity (generative text confusion); Min-K% Probability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> (focusing on the lowest-probability tokens; <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}0,10,20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}0,10,20</annotation></semantics></math>); and Max Rényi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> (Rényi entropy-based; <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.5,1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.5,1.0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}0,10,100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}0,10,100</annotation></semantics></math>).
We report AUROC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and TPR@0.05FPR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2 </span>Results</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.2.2 Results ‣ 6.2 Experiment 2: Benchmark Evaluation ‣ 6 Experimental Evaluation ‣ OpenLVLM-MIA: A Controlled Benchmark Revealing the Limits of Membership Inference Attacks on Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows MIA performance on OpenLVLM-MIA.
Under the bias-controlled setting, we evaluate ten methods across three training phases (Vision Encoder, Projector, Instruction Tuning) and four logit slices (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">img</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">inst</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">desp</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">inst+desp</span>).
Across all conditions, AUROC lies in the narrow range 0.407–0.527, at best only slightly above chance (0.5).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MIA performance (AUROC) on OpenLVLM-MIA. Bold: highest; underlined: lowest per column.</span></figcaption>
